Amine-Functionalized Mesoporous Silicas. Early efforts to incorporate amines into porous materials focused primarily on mesoporous silicas. With BET surface areas ranging from 500 to $1\hat{5}00\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ a high density of amines can be impregnated inside or grafted directly onto the pore surface of a mesoporous silica.64 While there has been some controversy as to the exact nature of adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ inside these materials, it is now generally accepted that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reacts to form an ammonium carbamate under dry conditions $(0.5\ \mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per amine) and ammonium bicarbonate (1 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per amine) under humid conditions, provided that the silica has a high density of $1^{\circ}$ or
$2^{\circ}$ amines.62b,65 It is important to note that although these are the thermodynamically favored products under each set of conditions, ammonium carbamate formation occurs at a much faster rate than bicarbonate formation.66 As a result, it can take a very long time to reach a full equilibrium capacity of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~1~CO}}_{2}$ per amine in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , as there is often a mixture of ammonium carbamate and bicarbonate present before equilibrium is reached. This perhaps explains the wide range of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to amine ratios that have been reported for different amineappended or -impregnated silicas in the presence of H2O.11a Additionally, the majority of reports on the equilibrium $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of mesoporous silicas in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ involve flowing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over a sample that has been pre-equilibrated with $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and measuring the increase in weight. To calculate the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake, it is assumed that the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorbed is unaffected by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Recent binary adsorption measurements, however, have shown that this assumption often leads to incorrect conclusions about the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .67
Many of the amine-functionalized silicas investigated for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture have been based on MCM-41 (Mobil composition of matter no. 41), which consists of cylindrical mesopores that are arranged in a hexagonal lattice.68 In this work, three aminefunctionalized derivatives of MCM-41 were synthesized according to previous reports: 3-aminopropyl grafted MCM41 $(1^{\circ}-\mathrm{MCM}-41)$ ), 3-methylaminopropyl grafted MCM-41 $(2^{\circ}.$ - MCM-41), and polyethylenimine impregnated MCM-41 (PEIMCM-41).69,70b Based on elemental analysis, $4.2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $1^{\circ}$ amine per $\mathbf{g}$ silica and $3.7\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $2^{\circ}$ amine per $\mathbf{g}$ silica were grafted in $1^{\circ}{\cdot}\mathrm{MCM}{\cdot}41\$ and $2^{\circ}{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{M}{\cdot}41\$ , respectively, which is comparable to the average loading for all reported monoamineappended silicas of just over $3\mathrm{\mmol/g.}^{11}$ a At $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 bar, both $1^{\circ}$ -MCM-41 and $2^{\circ}$ -MCM-41 adsorb nearly 0.5 molecules of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per amine, as expected for ammonium carbamate formation under dry conditions (Figure 11).
With $33\%$ of $\mathbf{N}$ by mass and a roughly 1:1:1 mixture $1^{\circ},2^{\circ}.$ , and $3^{\circ}$ amines, branched PEI has a high density of amine groups and has been shown to have improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture performance when it is impregnated inside a solid support rather than neat.70 The PEI-MCM-41 sample synthesized in this work contained 32 wt $\%$ of PEI $7.4\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{N}$ per $\mathbf{g}$ silica). At $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 bar, PEI-MCM-41 adsorbs 0.28 molecules of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per amine $\left(2.1\mathrm{\mmol/g}\right)$ , which is consistent with the amine efficiencies of previously reported amine-impregnated silicas (Figure 11).11
Here, multicomponent experiments were used to measure directly the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed from a mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ ${\bf N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ partial pressure near 0.15 bar. Note that since primary amines are prone to deactivate by forming urea linkages under dry conditions,69 the pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and multicomponent isotherms reported here were measured on separate samples. At an equilibrium $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ partial pressure of 19 mbar $26\%$ RH), the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed by $1^{\circ}$ -MCM41 decreased by $25\%$ . This result appears to be in conflict with an earlier report that used a thermogravimetric analyzer coupled to a mass spectrometer to show a $76\%$ increase in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption for $1^{\circ}{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{M}{\cdot}41\$ at 24 mbar of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 25 °C.67 The two 1°-MCM-41 samples have nearly identical amine loadings, but the bare MCM-41 silica used in this work has an average pore size of $2.4\ \mathrm{nm},$ while the previously reported material used a pore-enlarged variant of MCM-41 with an average pore size of nearly $\overset{\vartriangle}{11}\:\mathrm{nm}$ .71 The different pore sizes of the resulting amine-grafted materials could be responsible for the different $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities observed under humid conditions.
The amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed by $2^{\circ}$ -MCM-41 at 17 mbar of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was within error of the dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake, suggesting that no bicarbonate was formed. Although the sample was equilibrated for over $^{24}\mathrm{~h,}$ it is likely that bicarbonate formation is exceedingly slow in this secondary amine material and equilibrium was not reached over the course of the multicomponent measurement.66 During the multicomponent measurement, the total pressure of the system was still decreasing at a very slow rate even after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of forced equilibration, which suggests that some $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was still occurring with very slow kinetics. There are several literature reports of constant $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake for amine-appended silicas under both dry and humid conditions, but whether or not kinetics are a factor in these experiments is uncertain.11a
In contrast to $1^{\circ}{\cdot}\mathrm{MCM-}41$ and $2^{\circ}$ -MCM-41, the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed in PEI-MCM-41 increased significantly from 1.5 $\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ $\mathrm{~\'~}0.20\mathrm{~CO}_{2}$ per N) to $2.5(1)\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ ( $0.34~\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per N) at 17 mbar of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . This $67\%$ increase is slightly higher than the $40\%$ increase observed for 50 wt $\%$ PEI-MCM-41 in a dynamic breakthrough experiment at $75~^{\circ}C.^{70\mathrm{c}}$ Although the observed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity is ${<}1\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per amine, it is possible that either bicarbonate is still forming but the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ partial pressure is not high enough to reach a full capacity of 1 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ per amine or that not all of the amine sites of the PEI are accessible to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
Amine-Appended Metal−Organic Frameworks. While initial efforts to functionalize solid adsorbents with amines involved mostly mesoporous silicas, several amine-appended metal−organic frameworks have recently shown promising $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture properties.72 For instance, the amine-appended metal−organic framework $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{\lbrack(Cu_{4}C l)_{3}(B T T r i)_{8}(m m e n)_{12}\rbrack}$ (mmen-CuBTTri; $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTTri}}&{{}=1,3,5\mathrm{-tri}\left(1H\mathrm{-}1,2,3\mathrm{-triazo}\right)}\end{array}$ l-4-yl)- benzene) features diamines that are coordinated to the exposed ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ cations of its sodalite-type cages (Figure 1), resulting in a nearly $250\%$ increase in the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed at 0.15 bar and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as compared to the bare framework.72c Similar to $1^{\circ}$ -MCM-41, mmen-CuBTTri shows a very slight decrease in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake in the presence of ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ but still adsorbs 0.9(2) $\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Table S3). This demonstrates that appending amines to exposed metal cation sites in metal− organic frameworks is a viable strategy for adsorbing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under humid conditions.
In contrast to mesoporous silicas, highly crystalline metal− organic frameworks offer the advantage of well-defined and ordered sites for attaching amines, a feature that was recently shown to lead to unprecedented cooperative $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding and low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture regeneration energies in the diamineappended metal−organic framework mmen- $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ .73 In this material, which features an expanded version of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ , one end of each diamine is attached to an exposed $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}$ cation, while the other end is exposed on the surface (Figure 1). With all diamines spaced exactly $6.8\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ apart along each hexagonal channel, the density of amine groups in mmen- $\mathbf{\cdotMg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ is $7.3\ \mathrm{mmol/mL},$ exceeding that of a 30 wt $\%$ monoethanolamine solution $\left(4.9\mathrm{\mmol/mL}\right)$ ).
At $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and pressures below 0.5 mbar, the mechanism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in mmen- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{g}_{2}}$ (dobpdc) is thought to be similar to that of classical amine-appended silicas: two free amine groups react with a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule to form ammonium carbamate (Figure 12). At higher pressures, however, there is a sharp step in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of mmen$\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ , and both ends of the diamine begin to participate in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding. Specifically, a proton is transferred from a metal-bound amine to a neighboring free amine as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ simultaneously inserts into the $\mathrm{{\calMg-N}}$ bond, resulting in the formation of one-dimensional chains of ammonium carbamate that run parallel to each chain of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ cations.73 This insertionbased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption mechanism leads to strong, cooperative $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding, and at 0.15 bar and $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ one $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule is adsorbed per mmen to give a total capacity of $3.73\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ (14.1 wt $\%$ ).
Since $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ but not ${\bf N}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , induces a phase transition in mmen- $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ , changing both the structure and composition of the adsorbent, it is impossible to use purecomponent adsorption isotherms to predict $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity and capacity for a mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Instead, the direct measurement of multicomponent adsorption is the only way to investigate the effects of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding in mmen- $\mathrm{\dot{M}g}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ at equilibrium. Of particular interest is to determine whether the insertion-based $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption mechanism is affected by $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and if ammonium carbamate is still the only form of adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under humid conditions.
Significantly, multicomponent measurements with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ partial pressures as high as 19 mbar, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ partial pressures between 100 and 300 mbar, and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ partial pressures between 500 and 700 mbar, clearly show that the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed from a mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is the same as, or slightly higher than, from pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Thus, $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ do not have a significant effect on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in mmen$\mathrm{{\calMg}}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ (Figure 13a). To the best of our knowledge, the multicomponent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $4.2(2)\mathrm{mmol/g}\left(15.6\mathrm{wt}\%\right)$ for mmen- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{g}_{2}}$ (dobpdc) represents the highest value ever reported for any metal−organic framework or silica under similar humid conditions. While no $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption is observed in mmen$\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ during the multicomponent experiments, between 4.9 and $9.5\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is adsorbed, depending on the exact equilibrium conditions. Since $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ do not compete for the same binding sites, it should be possible to reduce this $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption without affecting the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed by, for instance, tuning the alkylamine or bridging organic ligand to increase the hydrophobicity of the pore surface. Note that one of the multicomponent measurements was repeated four further times on the same sample with identical results after regenerating at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $^{2\ \mathrm{h},}$ demonstrating that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption is fully reversible (Figure 5). Longer cycling studies with mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and regeneration using a pure temperature swing are currently in progress.
To confirm that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ insertion into the $\bf{M g-N}$ bond is still occurring in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ infrared spectroscopy measurements were performed after equilibrating a sample of mmen- $\cdot\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{\ensuremath{g}}_{2}}$ (dobpdc) with a mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\ \mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure 14). Previously, a sharp band was observed at 1334 $\mathsf{c m}^{\mathsf{-1}}$ in the infrared spectrum of mmen- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{g}_{2}}$ (dobpdc) dosed with pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . This band was assigned to the $\mathrm{C-N}$ stretch of a carbamate and is only present after insertion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into the $\mathbf{M}{-}\mathbf{N}$ bond generates an O-bound carbamate species with more double-bond character in the $\mathrm{C-N}$ bond.73 Significantly, there is also a sharp band observed at $1334~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ for mmen$\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobpdc}\big)$ in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},\mathrm{N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , confirming that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ insertion into the $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-N}}$ bond is still occurring and that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ does not change the mechanism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption (Figure 14). Although the multicomponent measurements indicate a very slight increase in the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , there is no evidence of any carbonate or bicarbonate species in the infrared spectrum.
In the isostructural nickel analogue of this framework, mmen$\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})_{\mathrm{:}}$ , the increased strength of the $\mathrm{{Ni-N}}$ bond makes the insertion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ thermodynamically unfavorable, and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms do not contain a step, resulting in just $0.4\mathrm{mmol/g}$ $\left(1.7\mathrm{wt}\ \%\right)$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 0.15 bar.73 Note that while the pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherm of mmen$\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ is not nearly as steep as expected for an adsorbent with strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding sites, the isosteric heat of adsorption at low coverage is still $-40$ to $-60\mathrm{kJ/mol}.$ The isotherm is not as steep as that of most metal−organic frameworks with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding energies in this range, such as $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , because the entropy of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption is much more positive in mmen$\mathbf{Ni}_{2}$ (dobpdc) than in more classical materials.73 This strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding and large entropy of adsorption can likely be explained by a similar mechanism as for the pre-step adsorption in mmen- $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc): a proton is transferred between the free ends of two neighboring diamines and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is bound via the formation of ammonium carbamate, resulting in one $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ bound per every two mmen (Figure 12). In order for this proton transfer to occur, the diamines likely lose some rotational degrees of freedom as ammonium carbamate pairs are formed, which leads to the large entropy penalty associated with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding.
To support the proposed mechanism for dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in mmen- $\mathbf{\cdotNi_{2}}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ , infrared spectroscopy measurements were performed on mmen- $\cdot\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ in the presence of pure ${\bf N}_{2}$ and pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 14). Under a pure $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, two distinct $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ vibrations are observed at 3330 and 3258 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and can be assigned to the free and Ni-bound ends of the diamines, respectively. The metal-bound $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretch is identical to that observed for mmen- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc), while the free $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ stretch is red-shifted by $4~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ .73 Upon exposure to 1 bar of pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ the free $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ resonance at $3\hat{3}30~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ disappears, and a new vibration is observed at $3271~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The disappearance of the free $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ vibration is consistent with the formation of ammonium. If only the free ends of each diamine participate in the reaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ then half of all diamines would be expected to have an ammonium on the free end, while the other half of diamines would have a carbamate (Figure 12). Indeed, there are two $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ resonances of roughly equal intensity observed at 3271 and $3258~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ that can be assigned to the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ on Ni-bound ends of diamines that have either an ammonium or a carbamate on the free end. A broad feature also grows in at roughly $1680~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dosing, which is consistent with the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ vibration of carbamate. Finally, no new bands are observed between 1275 and $1375~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, which is consistent with the ammonium carbamate reaction occurring at the free ends of the diamines, with no insertion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into the $\mathrm{\Delta\M-Ni}$ bond.
Somewhat unexpectedly, the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed in mmen- $\mathbf{\cdotNi}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpd}\bar{\mathrm{c}})$ increases nearly 3-fold to 1.3(2) $\mathrm{mmol/g}$ (5.4 wt $\%$ ) in the presence of 29 mbar of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 691 mbar of ${\bf N}_{2}$ (Figure 13b). This substantial increase in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption is greater than typically observed for amine-appended silicas and suggests that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has a strong effect on the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding mechanism. Despite the large increase in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake, only 0.37(7) mmol of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is adsorbed per mmol of free amine under the multicomponent conditions.
To gain further insight into these multicomponent results, infrared spectroscopy measurements were also performed for mmen- ${\bf\cdot N i}_{2}$ (dobpdc) under an atmosphere of ${\bf N}_{2},$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ (Figure 14). Unfortunately, a very broad and high intensity band above $3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , resulting from the large amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the gas-phase, overwhelms any $\mathrm{{N-H}}$ resonances (Figures S18, S19). Still, no new bands are observed between 1275 and $1375~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , demonstrating that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ insertion into the $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{Ni}}$ bond does not occur in mmen$\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ even in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Although $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ insertion is ruled out, the infrared spectrum of mmen$\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})$ does not provide any conclusive evidence that can explain the increased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . It is possible that bicarbonate is formed, but its infrared bands are masked by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or the organic bridging ligand. Alternatively, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ may facilitate the formation of more ammonium carbamate at lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ partial pressures by, for instance, breaking up hydrogen-bonding between neighboring diamines to expose more $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding sites than are present under dry conditions.65g
## CONCLUSIONS
The multicomponent results reported in this work for adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ ${\bf N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in 15 different materials, including metal−organic frameworks, zeolites, mesoporous silicas, and an activated carbon, are summarized in Figure 15.
Here, a multicomponent data point is plotted for each material at the equilibrium conditions most representative of the composition of a coal-fired power plant flue gas. In the bottom pane of the bar graph, the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed from the multicomponent mixture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ ${\bf N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (red) is compared to the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed at the same partial pressure in the absence of $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (green). In the top pane, the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorbed in the multicomponent experiment is shown. As has been discussed in detail, all of the adsorbents with exposed metal cations or anions take up a significant amount of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and negligible amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under these simulated flue gas equilibrium conditions, while MOF-5 and the AX-21 activated carbon have mostly nonpolar surfaces that lead not only to very little $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption but also to very little $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at these low pressures. Adsorbents functionalized with alkylamines, however, maintain a significant $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity at the multicomponent equilibrium conditions, with mmen- $\cdot\mathrm{Mg}_{2}$ (dobpdc) adsorbing a record amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
In addition to maximizing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, minimizing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption is critical to achieving low regeneration energies in any $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture process that involves a temperature swing since some of the heat supplied to desorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ will also go toward desorbing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The direct measurement of equilibrium $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption in the presence of ${\bf N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is thus important for identifying adsorbents with the best $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture performance. This is particularly true for adsorbents that change upon binding $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ where $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption may be very different before and after $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. It is worth noting that measuring the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorbed in a breakthrough experiment with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is extremely challenging, if not impossible, when the column is pre-equilibrated with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ as is often necessary when running small-scale breakthrough experiments.
Although the multicomponent results presented in this work targeted equilibrium conditions representative of adsorption from a post-combustion flue gas, evaluating multicomponent equilibria at desorption conditions is also important. Since a pure temperature swing or vacuum-assisted temperature swing process will likely be most effective for CO2 capture,8,12c evaluating the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorbed from mixtures as a function of regeneration temperature and pressure is necessary to determine $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ working capacities and to calculate regeneration energies. Recently, it was shown that the cooperative $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding in mmen- $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobpdc) allows a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ working capacity of nearly 14 wt $\%$ $\left(3.7\mathrm{\mmol/g}\right)$ to be achieved with an unprecedented temperature swing of just 50 °C.73 The effects of H2O on this working capacity are currently being investigated under both dynamic and equilibrium conditions. Because of their structural and chemical tunability, metal−organic frameworks functionalized with alkylamines are a particularly promising class of solid adsorbents for achieving high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ working capacities, minimal $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption, and low regeneration energies at post-combustion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture conditions.
In addition to $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ ${\bf N}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , multicomponent measurements and long-term cycling studies that include other flue gas components, such as $\mathrm{O}_{2},\mathrm{NO}_{x},$ and $\mathrm{SO}_{x},$ will be valuable in assessing adsorbents for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture applications. Measuring multicomponent equilibrium kinetics and heats of adsorption, which are particularly challenging experiments, is also important but rarely reported in the literature for gas mixture s.17b While no real CO2 capture process will operate under true equilibrium conditions, equilibrium data are essential for comparing different materials and for simulating the dynamic processes of a large-scale separation.
